      "text": "To find the sum of all the positive divisors of 91, we first need to determine its prime factorization. We can start by checking if 91 is divisible by small prime numbers.\n\n91 is not divisible by 2 (it's an odd number).\n91 is not divisible by 3 (the sum of its digits, 9 + 1 = 10, is not divisible by 3).\n91 is not divisible by 5 (it does not end in 0 or 5).\n91 is divisible by 7 (91 ÷ 7 = 13).\n\nSo, the prime factorization of 91 is 7 × 13.\n\nNow, we can use this factorization to find the sum of all the positive divisors. Since 91 has only two distinct prime factors, we can use the formula for the sum of divisors:\n\nSum of divisors = (p1^(a1 + 1) - 1)/(p1 - 1) × (p2^(a2 + 1) - 1)/(p2 - 1)\n\nwhere p1 and p2 are the prime factors, and a1 and a2 are their respective powers in the factorization.\n\nIn our case, p1 = 7, a1 = 1, p2 = 13, and a2 = 1.\n\nSum of divisors = (7^(1 + 1) - 1)/(7 - 1) × (13^(1 + 1) - 1)/(13 - 1)\n                 = (7^2 - 1)/(7 - 1) × (13^2 - 1)/(13 - 1)\n                 = (49 - 1)/6 × (169 - 1)/12\n                 = 48/6 × 168/12\n                 = 8 × 14\n                 = 112\n\nSo, the sum of all the positive divisors of 91 is 112.",
